<resources>
    <!--
      The native half of the theme. These must track src/common/theme/colors.ts.

      `app_background` is `bg` — the colour of the window behind React, so it is
      what shows during an activity transition. `bootsplash_background` is
      `splashTop`, which the JS splash paints over.

      **The two are not the same value in light mode, and v6 widened the gap.**
      v4 splashed on a warm cream a shade off its field. v6 splashes on the
      *brand*: its splash screen is the masthead wave at full height, so the top
      of the screen is the front wave layer `#7A3EA1` (palette.plum500) while the
      app behind it is the lilac wash `#F6EFFA` (palette.lilac50). Keep them
      apart: setting bootsplash_background to the field colour reintroduces the
      seam at the top of the screen that scripts/check-splash-colour.sh exists to
      catch — and here that seam would be a full purple-to-lilac jump rather than
      v4's barely-visible cream step.

      values-night/ carries the dark pair. Note what that can and cannot do: the
      OS night setting is the only signal available before React starts, so these
      follow the *device*, not the app's Light/Dark/System preference — which
      lives in MMKV and cannot be read this early. A user who forces Light on a
      dark device therefore gets a dark splash and a light app. That mismatch is
      one frame at the handoff; the alternative was a bright flash on every dark
      cold start, which is worse and was the previous behaviour.
    -->
    <color name="app_background">#F6EFFA</color>
    <color name="bootsplash_background">#7A3EA1</color>
</resources>
